ce-open-library-john-brown-s-war-against-slavery-robert-e-mcglone"}],"publicationYear":2013,"pageCount":0,"summary":"Drawing on both new and neglected evidence, this book reconstructs Old John Brown's aborted \"war\" to free the 3.8 million slaves in the American South before the Civil War. It critiques misleading sources that either exalt Brown's \"heroism\" and noble purpose or condemn his \"monomania\" and \"lawlessness\". McGlone explains the sources of his obsession with slavery and his notorious crime at Pottawatomie Creek in \"Bleeding Kansas\" as well as how the Harpers Ferry raid figured into Brown's larger vision and why he was captured in the federal armory there. John Brown's War Against Slavery chronicles how this aged American apostle of violence in behalf of the \"downtrodden,\" this abolitionist \"fanatic\" and \"terroriser,\" ultimately rescued his cause by going to the gallows with resolution and outward calm.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-john-brown-s-war-against-slavery-robert-e-mcglone.jpg","displaySummary":"Drawing on both new and neglected evidence, this book reconstructs Old John Brown's aborted \"war\" to free the 3.8 million slaves in the American South before the Civil War. It critiques misleading sources that either exalt Brown's \"heroism\" and noble purpose or condemn his \"monomania\" and \"lawlessness\". McGlone explains the sources of his obsession with slavery and his notorious crime at Pottawatomie Creek in \"Bleeding Kansas\" as well as how the Harpers Ferry raid figured into Brown's larger vision and why he was captured in the federal armory there. John Brown's War Against Slavery chronicles how this aged American apostle of violence in behalf of the \"downtrodden,\" this abolitionist \"fanatic\" and \"terroriser,\" ultimately rescued his cause by going to the gallows with resolution and outward calm.","primarySubject":"Biography","primaryTopic":"American Civil War","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Biography","confidence